1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,550
Chapter 49: General Considerations and Maternal.

3
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:09,200
Evaluation Medications.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,662
Nonobstetrical Surgery.

5
00:00:11,662 --> 00:00:14,050
Laparoscopic Surgery.

6
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:16,012
Radiography.

7
00:00:16,012 --> 00:00:18,462
Diagnostic Radiation.

8
00:00:18,462 --> 00:00:20,325
Sonography.

9
00:00:20,325 --> 00:00:22,900
Magnetic Resonance Imaging.

10
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:24,662
References.

11
00:00:24,662 --> 00:00:31,412
Pregnant women are susceptible to any medical and surgical disorder that can affect women of childbearing age.

12
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:38,950
Chronic illnesses often precede pregnancy, and an acute condition can complicate an otherwise normal pregnancy.

13
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:44,325
Both chronic and acute disorders raise the risk for antepartum hospitalization.

14
00:00:44,325 --> 00:00:56,062
Approximately 10 per 100 pregnant women incur an antepartum admission, and one third are for nonobstetrical conditions that include renal, pulmonary, and infectious diseases .

15
00:00:56,062 --> 00:01:02,312
The hospitalization rate due to trauma approximates 4 admissions per 1000 deliveries .

16
00:01:02,312 --> 00:01:08,425
Those with intellectual and developmental disabilities have a higher incidence of hospitalization .

17
00:01:08,425 --> 00:01:14,587
Last, 1 to 2 percent of pregnant women will undergo a nonobstetrical surgical procedure .

18
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:21,387
Obstetricians should have a working knowledge of the wide-ranging medical disorders common to childbearing-aged women.

19
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:25,437
Many of these are within the purview of the general obstetrician.

20
00:01:25,437 --> 00:01:34,812
Other disorders, however, will warrant consultation with specialists in maternal-fetal medicine, and still others require a multidisciplinary team.

21
00:01:34,812 --> 00:01:41,687
The latter may include internists and medical subspecialists, surgeons, and anesthesiologists .

22
00:01:41,687 --> 00:01:51,887
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2014) has redefined aspects of maternal care and proposed conditions requiring specialized care.

23
00:01:51,887 --> 00:01:55,825
Treatment should never be withheld because a woman is pregnant.

24
00:01:55,825 --> 00:02:05,237
To ensure this and allow for individualized care, several questions must be addressed: • What management would be recommended if the woman were not pregnant?

25
00:02:05,237 --> 00:02:10,850
If the proposed management is different because the woman is pregnant, can this be justified?

26
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:16,337
What are the risks and benefits to the mother and her fetus, and are they counter to each other?

27
00:02:16,337 --> 00:02:22,200
Can an individualized management plan be devised that balances risks versus benefits?

28
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,212
Maternal Physiology And Laboratory.

29
00:02:25,212 --> 00:02:26,912
Values.

30
00:02:26,912 --> 00:02:32,225
Pregnancy induces physiological changes in virtually all organ systems.

31
00:02:32,225 --> 00:02:37,662
Many of these are discussed in Chapter 4 and in the subsequent chapters in this section.

32
00:02:37,662 --> 00:02:42,662
In turn, numerous laboratory test results also are normally altered.

33
00:02:42,662 --> 00:02:47,062
Some values would be considered abnormal in the nonpregnant woman.

34
00:02:47,062 --> 00:02:53,512
Conversely, some may appear to be within a normal range but are decidedly abnormal for the gravida.

35
00:02:53,512 --> 00:02:58,512
These changes may complicate the evaluation of coexisting conditions.

36
00:02:58,512 --> 00:03:06,175
To aid interpretation, normal laboratory values in pregnancy are listed in the Appendix (p. 1227).

37
00:03:06,175 --> 00:03:08,112
Medications.

38
00:03:08,112 --> 00:03:16,000
Fortunately, most medications needed to treat frequently encountered illnesses in pregnancy can be given with relative safety .

39
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,575
That said, notable exceptions are considered in Chapter 8 and throughout this text.

40
00:03:21,575 --> 00:03:35,387
The risks and benefits of medication use during pregnancy and lactation are outlined in drug labels using the Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule (PLLR) requirement from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA).

41
00:03:35,387 --> 00:03:37,850
Nonobstetrical Surgery.

42
00:03:37,850 --> 00:03:48,975
The chances of adverse maternal and perinatal outcomes following nonobstetrical surgery during pregnancy are relatively low and cannot be separated from risks of the underlying condition .

43
00:03:48,975 --> 00:03:53,062
However, risks are likely greater with complications.

44
00:03:53,062 --> 00:04:06,587
Compared with simple appendicitis, perforated appendicitis with feculent peritonitis has significantly higher maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality rates even if surgical and anesthetic techniques are flawless.

45
00:04:06,587 --> 00:04:12,087
Moreover, procedurerelated complications may adversely affect outcomes.

46
00:04:12,087 --> 00:04:22,812
For example, a woman who has uncomplicated removal of an inflamed appendix may suffer aspiration of acidic gastric contents during tracheal intubation or extubation.

47
00:04:22,812 --> 00:04:25,425
# Maternal Morbidity.

48
00:04:25,425 --> 00:04:37,900
The most frequent nonobstetrical surgical procedures performed during pregnancy are appendectomy, cholecystectomy, and adnexal surgery (Vujic, 2019; Yu, 2018).

49
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:42,825
Postoperative complications in nonpregnant patients can similarly harm

50
00:04:42,988 --> 00:04:44,700
gravid women.

51
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:49,963
However, data comparing these complications in these two groups are conflicting.

52
00:04:49,963 --> 00:05:00,013
The National Surgical Quality Improvement Program showed that morbidity and mortality rates from nonobstetrical surgery did not differ between pregnant and nonpregnant women .

53
00:05:00,013 --> 00:05:12,575
In a Taiwanese cohort of nearly 5600 gravidas, the infectious postoperative complication rate was slightly higher and the mortality rate was fourfold greater than those rates in nonpregnant women .

54
00:05:12,575 --> 00:05:26,050
The reported postoperative complication rate in gravidas approximates 5 percent, and the maternal mortality rate in these cases is <1 percent (Huang, 2016; Vujic, 2019).

55
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:33,575
Moreover, preoperative infection, specifically sepsis, is associated with a higher risk of maternal death .

56
00:05:33,575 --> 00:05:36,313
# Perinatal Morbidity.

57
00:05:36,313 --> 00:05:46,638
Excessive perinatal morbidity associated with nonobstetrical surgery is also attributable in many cases to the disease itself rather than to surgery and anesthesia.

58
00:05:46,638 --> 00:05:56,113
Two- to threefold elevated risks for spontaneous abortion, preterm delivery, preeclampsia, and cesarean delivery have been reported .

59
00:05:56,113 --> 00:06:11,638
Balinskaite and associates (2017) identified a greater risk of fetal death, preterm birth, fetal-growth restriction, and cesarean delivery in 47,628 women undergoing nonobstetrical surgery.

60
00:06:11,638 --> 00:06:16,338
Most of these complications developed in cases performed emergently .

61
00:06:16,338 --> 00:06:25,875
The Swedish Birth Registry provides valuable data comparing perinatal outcomes in women undergoing surgery with those of the general obstetrical population .

62
00:06:25,875 --> 00:06:33,600
The incidences of congenital malformations and stillbirth were not significantly different from those of nonexposed newborns.

63
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:40,475
However, incidences of low birthweight, preterm birth, and neonatal death were significantly higher.

64
00:06:40,475 --> 00:06:45,038
Increased neonatal death rates were largely due to prematurity.

65
00:06:45,038 --> 00:06:47,613
Table 49-1.

66
00:06:47,613 --> 00:06:58,600
Birth Outcomes in 5405 Pregnant Women Undergoing Nonobstetrical Surgery Fetal abnormality rates are not associated with maternal surgery in early pregnancy.

67
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:09,638
Källén and Mazze (1990) reported a nonsignificant relationship between elevated neural-tube defect rates and operations performed at 4 to 5 weeks’ gestation.

68
00:07:09,638 --> 00:07:15,488
A large database study found no evidence that anesthetic agents were teratogenic .

69
00:07:15,488 --> 00:07:22,588
According to Briggs and coworkers (2017), most inhalation anesthetic agents appear safe.

70
00:07:22,588 --> 00:07:30,913
More recently, concerns of neurodevelopmental harm with the use of anesthetics for obstetrical or fetal surgery have been raised.

71
00:07:30,913 --> 00:07:45,588
In 2016, the FDA issued a warning regarding impaired brain development in children following in utero exposure to inhaled isoflurane, sevoflurane, and desflurane as well as intravenous propofol and midazolam.

72
00:07:45,588 --> 00:07:50,288
Such risks appear to accrue after 3 hours or more of exposure .

73
00:07:50,288 --> 00:07:52,675
Laparoscopic Surgery.

74
00:07:52,675 --> 00:08:01,163
In the first trimester, laparoscopy is the most common procedure used for diagnosis and management of several surgical disorders.

75
00:08:01,163 --> 00:08:13,063
In 2017, the Society of American Gastrointestinal and Endoscopic Surgeons (SAGES) updated its recommendations concerning laparoscopy use in pregnant women .

76
00:08:13,063 --> 00:08:15,600
Table 49-2.

77
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:28,075
Some Guidelines from the Society of American Gastrointestinal Endoscopic Surgeons (SAGES) for Laparoscopic Surgery in Pregnant Women For pregnancy in general, laparotomy also is common.

78
00:08:28,075 --> 00:08:41,700
One 5-year study of almost 1300 pregnant women reported that open appendectomy was performed in 36 percent of 857 gravidas compared with only 17 percent of nonpregnant patients.

79
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:51,738
Of those undergoing cholecystectomy, an open approach was used in 10 percent of 436 pregnant women compared with 5 percent of nonpregnant women .

80
00:08:51,738 --> 00:09:06,538
In a study from Japan, Shigemi and colleagues (2019) described 6018 pregnant women undergoing abdominal surgery—4047 by laparotomy and 1971 by laparoscopy.

81
00:09:06,538 --> 00:09:15,188
Operative times, hospital stay lengths, and rates of adverse fetal events and blood transfusion were all greater in the laparotomy group .

82
00:09:15,188 --> 00:09:23,763
Others instead report equally satisfactory outcomes with either approach (Cox, 2016; Lee, 2019).

83
00:09:23,763 --> 00:09:37,875
Randomized trials to compare benefits and risks of laparoscopy versus laparotomy during pregnancy are needed, but implementation seems unfeasible (Bunyavejchevin, 2013; Lee, 2019).

84
00:09:38,044 --> 00:09:40,619
Table 49-3.

85
00:09:40,569 --> 00:09:56,319
Comparative Outcomes in Pregnant Women Undergoing Abdominal Surgery For adnexal mass surgery in pregnancy, laparoscopy is preferred, and several studies confirm its relative safety (Daykan, 2016; Webb, 2015).

86
00:09:56,319 --> 00:10:09,556
In addition, laparoscopic splenectomy, adrenalectomy, and nephrectomy also have been described in pregnant women (Asizare, 2014; Dong, 2014; Miller, 2012).

87
00:10:09,556 --> 00:10:16,106
When first used, 26 to 28 weeks became the upper gestational-age limit recommended.

88
00:10:16,106 --> 00:10:23,569
However, as experience has accrued, many now describe laparoscopic surgery performed in the third trimester .

89
00:10:23,569 --> 00:10:32,481
In one report, a third of gravidas undergoing laparoscopic cholecystectomy or appendectomy were >26 weeks’ gestation .

90
00:10:32,481 --> 00:10:40,519
No serious adverse sequelae are linked to these procedures, and laparoscopy can safely be performed in all trimesters.

91
00:10:40,519 --> 00:10:43,444
# Hemodynamic Effects.

92
00:10:43,444 --> 00:10:50,669
Precise effects of laparoscopy in the human fetus are unknown, but animal investigations are informative.

93
00:10:50,669 --> 00:10:59,494
During laparoscopy, required abdominal insufflation causes hemodynamic changes that are summarized in Table 49-4.

94
00:10:59,494 --> 00:11:07,769
Reedy and associates (1995) studied baboons at the human equivalent of 22 to 26 weeks’ gestation.

95
00:11:07,769 --> 00:11:21,756
No substantive physiological changes were found with insufflation pressures of 10 mm Hg, but 20 mm Hg caused significant maternal cardiovascular and respiratory changes after 20 minutes.

96
00:11:21,756 --> 00:11:32,269
These included faster respiratory rate, respiratory acidosis, diminished cardiac output, and increased pulmonary artery and capillary wedge pressures.

97
00:11:32,269 --> 00:11:45,269
In pregnant ewes, uteroplacental blood flow declines when intraperitoneal insufflation pressure exceeded 15 mm Hg (Barnard, 1995; Hunter, 1995).

98
00:11:45,269 --> 00:11:50,831
This stemmed from decreased perfusion pressure and elevated placental vessel resistance .

99
00:11:50,831 --> 00:11:53,431
Table 49-4.

100
00:11:53,431 --> 00:12:06,381
Physiological Effects of CO2 Insufflation of the Peritoneal Cavity In women, cardiorespiratory changes are generally not severe if insufflation pressures remain <15 mm Hg.

101
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:18,631
Despite maintaining these low insufflation pressures in women at midpregnancy, the cardiac index dropped 26 percent after 5 minutes of insufflation and 21 percent after 15 minutes .

102
00:12:18,631 --> 00:12:26,219
Even so, mean arterial pressures, systemic vascular resistance, and heart rate did not change significantly.

103
00:12:26,219 --> 00:12:28,381
# Technique.

104
00:12:28,381 --> 00:12:32,944
The following is an overview of laparoscopic technique in pregnancy.

105
00:12:32,944 --> 00:12:41,306
For a detailed description and illustrations refer to Chapter 15 in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition .

106
00:12:41,306 --> 00:12:46,294
Preparation for laparoscopy differs little from that used for laparotomy.

107
00:12:46,294 --> 00:12:52,531
Bowel cleansing is not needed but may aid visualization and manipulations by emptying the colon.

108
00:12:52,531 --> 00:12:59,581
Nasogastric or orogastric decompression reduces the risk of stomach trocar puncture and aspiration.

109
00:12:59,581 --> 00:13:04,794
Aortocaval compression is avoided by a left-lateral tilt of the patient’s body.

110
00:13:04,794 --> 00:13:14,306
Positioning of the lower extremities in boot-type stirrups maintains access to the vagina for fetal sonographic assessment or manual uterine displacement.

111
00:13:14,306 --> 00:13:18,031
Intrauterine manipulators are logically avoided.

112
00:13:18,031 --> 00:13:27,856
Pregnancy-related hypercoagulability combined with pneumoperitoneum-associated, lower-extremity venous stasis raises venous thromboembolism risks.

113
00:13:27,856 --> 00:13:33,781
Prophylactic pneumatic compression devices are wrapped around the calves during patient positioning.

114
00:13:33,781 --> 00:13:46,331
Most reports describe the use of general endotracheal anesthesia with monitoring of end-tidal carbon dioxide—EtCO2 (Hong, 2006; Ribic-Pucelj, 2007).

115
00:13:46,331 --> 00:13:53,544
With controlled ventilation, EtCO2 is maintained between 30 and 35 mm Hg.

116
00:13:53,544 --> 00:14:02,956
Beyond the first trimester, technical modifications of standard pelvic laparoscopic entry are required to avoid uterine puncture or laceration .

117
00:14:02,956 --> 00:14:10,194
Many recommend open entry techniques to avoid perforations of the uterus, pelvic vessels, and adnexa.

118
00:14:10,194 --> 00:14:22,619
With the method described by Hasson (1971, 1974), the abdomen is incised at or above the umbilicus, and the peritoneal cavity entered under direct visualization .

119
00:14:22,619 --> 00:14:30,456
At this point, the cannula is connected to the insufflation system, and a 12–mm Hg pneumoperitoneum is created.

120
00:14:30,456 --> 00:14:37,269
The initial insufflation should be conducted slowly to allow for prompt assessment and reversal of any untoward

121
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:39,720
pressure-related effects.

122
00:14:39,670 --> 00:14:45,532
Gas leakage around the cannula is managed by tightening the surrounding skin with a towel clamp.

123
00:14:45,532 --> 00:14:52,695
Insertion of secondary trocars into the abdomen is most safely performed under direct laparoscopic viewing.

124
00:14:52,695 --> 00:14:56,195
Single-port surgery also has been described .

125
00:14:56,195 --> 00:14:58,782
Figure 49-1.

126
00:14:58,782 --> 00:15:06,632
Pregnant uterus at 10, 20, and 36 weeks’ gestation depicting distortion of other intraperitoneal organs.

127
00:15:06,632 --> 00:15:12,420
Reproduced with permission from Kho KA: Diagnostic and operative laparoscopy.

128
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:21,732
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

129
00:15:21,732 --> 00:15:26,220
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

130
00:15:26,220 --> 00:15:28,832
Figure 49-2.

131
00:15:28,832 --> 00:15:33,445
Hasson open entry technique for laparoscopic instrument placement.

132
00:15:33,445 --> 00:15:38,982
A. Fascia grasped with two Allis clamps and elevated prior to sharp incision.

133
00:15:38,982 --> 00:15:43,545
B. Two fascial stitches incorporate the peritoneum and fascia.

134
00:15:43,545 --> 00:15:49,795
C. These fascial sutures are wrapped around holders of the Hasson cannula to anchor it in place.

135
00:15:49,795 --> 00:15:55,582
Reproduced with permission from Kho KA: Diagnostic and operative laparoscopy.

136
00:15:55,582 --> 00:16:04,895
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

137
00:16:04,895 --> 00:16:09,382
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

138
00:16:09,382 --> 00:16:24,095
In more advanced pregnancies, direct entry through a left upper quadrant port in the midclavicular line, 2 cm beneath the costal margin, may better avoid the fundus (Donkervoort, 2011; Stepp, 2004).

139
00:16:24,095 --> 00:16:33,432
Known as the Palmer point, this entry site is also used in gynecological laparoscopy because visceroparietal adhesions infrequently form here .

140
00:16:33,432 --> 00:16:42,082
Gasless laparoscopy is a less commonly selected alternative approach that uses a rod with intraabdominal fan-blade-shaped retractors.

141
00:16:42,082 --> 00:16:46,520
When opened, these allow the abdominal wall to be lifted upward.

142
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:55,345
It avoids the typical laparoscopic cardiovascular changes because abdominal wall elevation is created by retraction rather than insufflation .

143
00:16:55,345 --> 00:16:57,820
# Complications.

144
00:16:57,820 --> 00:17:04,482
Risks inherent to any abdominal endoscopic procedure are probably slightly greater during pregnancy.

145
00:17:04,482 --> 00:17:14,620
The obvious unique one is perforation of the pregnant uterus with a trocar or Veress needle (Kizer, 2011; Mala, 2014).

146
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:23,532
That said, reported complications are infrequent (Choi, 2021; Koo, 2012; Post, 2019).

147
00:17:23,532 --> 00:17:26,320
# Perinatal Outcomes.

148
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:30,982
Perinatal outcomes in women are limited to observational studies.

149
00:17:30,982 --> 00:17:40,982
Reedy and colleagues (1997) used the updated Swedish Birth Registry database to analyze a 20-year period with more than 2 million deliveries.

150
00:17:40,982 --> 00:17:47,870
Of 2181 laparoscopic procedures, most were performed during the first trimester.

151
00:17:47,870 --> 00:17:55,745
Perinatal outcomes for these women were compared with those of all women in the database and those undergoing open surgical procedures.

152
00:17:55,745 --> 00:18:06,420
These investigators confirmed the earlier findings of an increased risk of low birthweight, preterm delivery, and fetal-growth restriction with surgery during pregnancy.

153
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:13,382
Differences were not found, however, in outcomes of women undergoing laparoscopy versus laparotomy.

154
00:18:13,382 --> 00:18:21,145
An observational study of 262 women undergoing surgery for an adnexal mass noted similar findings .

155
00:18:21,145 --> 00:18:29,720
Although the abortion and stillbirth rates are low with abdominal surgery, laparotomy has more adverse outcomes compared with laparoscopy .

156
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:31,682
Radiography.

157
00:18:31,682 --> 00:18:37,557
Imaging modalities are used as adjuncts for diagnosis and therapy during pregnancy.

158
00:18:37,557 --> 00:18:45,907
Options include sonography, radiography, computed tomography (CT), and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging.

159
00:18:45,907 --> 00:18:49,682
Of these, radiography is the most problematic.

160
00:18:49,682 --> 00:18:58,970
Inevitably, some radiographic procedures are performed before recognition of early pregnancy, usually because of trauma or serious illness .

161
00:18:58,970 --> 00:19:05,520
Fortunately, most diagnostic radiographic procedures are associated with minimal fetal risks.

162
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:16,832
However, as with medications, these procedures may lead to a needless therapeutic abortion because of patient or physician anxiety or to litigation if pregnancy outcome is adverse.

163
00:19:16,832 --> 00:19:25,707
The American College of Radiology (ACR) has addressed the growing concern of collective radiation doses in all fields of medicine .

164
00:19:25,707 --> 00:19:35,107
More recent publications have been in support of FDA efforts to decrease radiation exposure and to reduce the number of unnecessary examinations.

165
00:19:35,107 --> 00:19:37,170
Recommendations

166
00:19:37,324 --> 00:19:45,324
also include considerations for radiosensitive populations, such as children and pregnant or potentially pregnant women.

167
00:19:45,274 --> 00:19:50,111
At our institutions, special recommendations are made for gravidas.

168
00:19:50,111 --> 00:19:58,711
Radiation exposure values and duration are recorded and monitored in high-exposure areas such as CT and fluoroscopy units.

169
00:19:58,711 --> 00:20:02,986
Last, consultation with the radiologist is advised .

170
00:20:02,986 --> 00:20:05,886
# Ionizing Radiation.

171
00:20:05,886 --> 00:20:15,911
The term radiation refers to energy transmission and thus is applied to x-rays and also to microwaves, ultrasound, diathermy, and radio waves.

172
00:20:15,911 --> 00:20:23,299
Of these, xrays and gamma rays have short wavelengths with very high energy and are ionizing radiation forms.

173
00:20:23,299 --> 00:20:28,199
The other four energy forms have rather long wavelengths and low energy .

174
00:20:28,199 --> 00:20:40,886
Ionizing radiation can directly damage DNA or can create free hydroxyl radicals that in turn damage DNA (Hall, 1991; National Research Council, 1990).

175
00:20:40,886 --> 00:20:46,811
Methods of measuring the effects of x-rays are summarized in Table 49-5.

176
00:20:46,811 --> 00:20:56,461
The standard terms used are exposure (in air), dose (to tissue), and relative effective dose (to tissue accounting for biological effects).

177
00:20:56,461 --> 00:21:06,924
In the range of energies for diagnostic x-rays, the dose is now expressed in grays (Gy), and the relative effective dose is expressed in sieverts (Sv).

178
00:21:06,924 --> 00:21:09,774
These can be used interchangeably.

179
00:21:09,774 --> 00:21:21,761
For consistency, all doses discussed subsequently are expressed in currently used units of gray (1 Gy = 100 rad) or sievert (1 Sv = 100 rem).

180
00:21:21,761 --> 00:21:29,086
To convert, 1 Sv = 100 rem = 100 rad. Table 49-5.

181
00:21:29,086 --> 00:21:39,574
Some Measures of Ionizing Radiation As noted, x- and gamma-radiation at high doses can damage DNA, and this yields two biological effects in the fetus .

182
00:21:39,574 --> 00:21:43,649
These are deterministic effects and stochastic effects.

183
00:21:43,649 --> 00:21:46,561
# Deterministic Effects.

184
00:21:46,561 --> 00:21:57,324
One potential harm of radiation exposure is deterministic, which may result in abortion, growth restriction, congenital malformations, or intellectual disability.

185
00:21:57,324 --> 00:22:05,449
These deterministic effects are threshold effects, and the threshold level is the NOAEL—no observed adverse effect level .

186
00:22:05,449 --> 00:22:14,849
Although controversial, the NOAEL concept supports that there is no risk below the threshold dose of 0.05 Gy or 5 rad.

187
00:22:14,849 --> 00:22:22,961
It also suggests that the threshold for gross fetal malformations is more likely to be 0.2 Gy (20 rad) .

188
00:22:22,961 --> 00:22:31,461
The deterministic effects of ionizing radiation have been extensively studied for cell damage that leads to disordered embryogenesis.

189
00:22:31,461 --> 00:22:39,611
These have been assessed in animal models as well as in Japanese atomic bomb survivors and the Oxford Survey of Childhood Cancers .

190
00:22:39,611 --> 00:22:45,149
Other sources have confirmed prior observations and provide additional information .

191
00:22:45,149 --> 00:22:54,199
Animal Studies In the mouse model, the lethality risk is highest during the preimplantation period, which extends up to 10 days postconception .

192
00:22:54,199 --> 00:22:59,149
Blastomere destruction caused by chromosomal damage is the likely cause .

193
00:22:59,149 --> 00:23:10,011
During organogenesis, high-dose radiation—1 Gy or 100 rad—is more likely to cause malformations and growth restriction and less likely to be lethal.

194
00:23:10,011 --> 00:23:18,474
Studies of brain development suggest effects on neuronal development and a window of cortical sensitivity in early and midfetal periods.

195
00:23:18,474 --> 00:23:24,674
Instead, acute low-dose ionizing radiation appears to have no deleterious effects .

196
00:23:24,674 --> 00:23:37,999
Human Data Data on adverse human effects of high-dose ionizing radiation mostly derive from the atomic bomb survivors of Hiroshima and Nagasaki (Greskovich, 2000; Otake, 1987).

197
00:23:37,999 --> 00:23:50,586
The International Commission on Radiological Protection (2003) confirmed initial studies showing that the risk of severe intellectual disability was greatest between 8 and 15 weeks’ gestation .

198
00:23:50,586 --> 00:23:58,549
The mean decrease in intelligence quotient (IQ) scores was 25 points per Gy or 100 rad.

199
00:23:58,549 --> 00:24:04,186
The dose response appears linear, but it is unclear whether there is a threshold dose.

200
00:24:04,186 --> 00:24:16,874
At <8 weeks’ or >25 weeks’ gestation, a higher risk of intellectual disability in humans has not been documented, even with doses exceeding 0.5 Gy or 50 rad .

201
00:24:16,874 --> 00:24:22,961
Most estimates err on the conservative side by assuming a linear nonthreshold hypothesis.

202
00:24:22,961 --> 00:24:33,274
In a study of fetuses exposed to a low radiation dose in the first trimester, Guilbaud and colleagues (2019) did not find an increased risk

203
00:24:33,436 --> 00:24:38,423
for miscarriage, congenital anomalies, or fetal-growth restriction.

204
00:24:38,373 --> 00:24:41,023
Table 49-6.

205
00:24:41,023 --> 00:24:52,173
Deterministic Effects of Ionizing Radiation in Pregnancy Reports have described high-dose radiation used to treat women for malignancy, menorrhagia, and uterine myomas.

206
00:24:52,173 --> 00:25:08,011
Dekaban (1968) described 22 infants with microcephaly, intellectual disability, or both following exposure in the first half of pregnancy to an estimated 2.5 Gy or 250 rad for therapeutic radiation.

207
00:25:08,011 --> 00:25:11,836
These doses are also carcinogenic for the fetus .

208
00:25:11,836 --> 00:25:21,373
Summary of Fetal Radiation Exposure From 8 to 15 weeks’ gestation, the fetus is most susceptible to radiationinduced intellectual disability .

209
00:25:21,373 --> 00:25:26,686
Whether this is a threshold or nonthreshold linear function of dose is unresolved.

210
00:25:26,686 --> 00:25:43,423
The Committee on Biological Effects (1990) estimates the risk of severe intellectual disability to be as low as 4 percent for 0.1 Gy (10 rad) and as high as 60 percent for 1.5 Gy (150 rad).

211
00:25:43,423 --> 00:25:50,473
Recall that these doses are 2 to 100 times higher than those considered maximal from diagnostic radiation.

212
00:25:50,473 --> 00:25:59,136
Importantly, cumulative doses from multiple procedures may reach the harmful range, especially at 8 to 15 weeks’ gestation.

213
00:25:59,136 --> 00:26:07,948
At 16 to 25 weeks’ gestation, the risk is less, and there is no proven risk before 8 weeks or after 25 weeks .

214
00:26:07,948 --> 00:26:13,198
Embryofetal risks from low-dose diagnostic radiation appear to be minimal.

215
00:26:13,198 --> 00:26:25,973
Current evidence suggests that risks for malformations, growth restriction, or spontaneous abortion are not increased from a radiation dose of less than 0.05 Gy (5 rad).

216
00:26:25,973 --> 00:26:36,236
Brent (2009) concluded that gross congenital malformation rates would not be greater with exposure to less than 0.2 Gy (20 rad).

217
00:26:36,236 --> 00:26:46,373
Diagnostic radiographs seldom exceed 0.1 Gy (10 rad) and thus, these procedures are unlikely to cause deterministic effects .

218
00:26:46,373 --> 00:26:56,398
As emphasized by Groen and coworkers (2012), 0.1 Gy is the radiation equivalent to that from more than 1000 chest x-rays.

219
00:26:56,398 --> 00:26:59,123
# Stochastic Effects.

220
00:26:59,123 --> 00:27:06,973
These effects refer to random, presumably unpredictable oncogenic or mutagenic effects of radiation exposure.

221
00:27:06,973 --> 00:27:16,586
Stochastic effects concern associations between fetal diagnostic radiation exposure and increased risk of childhood cancers or genetic diseases.

222
00:27:16,586 --> 00:27:29,073
Excess cancers can result from in utero exposure to doses as low as 0.01 Sv or 1 rad (Doll, 1997; National Research Council, 2006).

223
00:27:29,073 --> 00:27:42,498
The estimated risk of childhood cancer following fetal exposure to 0.03 Gy or 3 rad doubles the background risk of 1 cancer in 600 exposed fetuses to that of 2 in 600 .

224
00:27:42,498 --> 00:27:51,123
In one report, in utero radiation exposure was linked to 10 solid cancers in adults from age 17 to 45 years.

225
00:27:51,123 --> 00:27:58,248
There was a dose-response relationship as previously noted at the 0.1 Sv or 10 rem threshold.

226
00:27:58,248 --> 00:28:05,861
These cancers likely are associated with a complex series of interactions between DNA and ionizing radiation.

227
00:28:05,861 --> 00:28:13,036
These interactions make it more problematic to predict cancer risk from low-dose radiation of less than 10 rem.

228
00:28:13,036 --> 00:28:28,236
Importantly, below doses of 0.1 to 0.2 Sv, evidence of a carcinogenic effect is not convincing (Brent, 2009, 2014; Preston, 2008; Strzelczyk, 2007).

229
00:28:28,236 --> 00:28:30,898
# X-Ray Dosimetry.

230
00:28:30,898 --> 00:28:40,048
Estimates of dose to the uterus and embryo for various frequently used radiographic examinations are summarized in Table 49-7.

231
00:28:40,048 --> 00:28:48,311
Imaging of maternal body parts farthest from the uterus results in a very small dose of radiation scatter to the embryo or fetus.

232
00:28:48,311 --> 00:28:54,336
The size of the woman, radiographic technique, and equipment performance are other variables .

233
00:28:54,336 --> 00:28:58,211
Thus, data in the table serve only as guidelines.

234
00:28:58,211 --> 00:29:04,936
When the radiation dose for a specific individual is required, a medical physicist should be consulted.

235
00:29:04,936 --> 00:29:10,511
The Health Physics Society lists answers to questions commonly asked by patients .

236
00:29:10,511 --> 00:29:13,136
Table 49-7.

237
00:29:13,136 --> 00:29:18,948
Dose to the Uterus for Common Radiologic Procedures # Therapeutic Radiation.

238
00:29:18,948 --> 00:29:29,536
The Radiation Therapy Committee Task Group of the American Association of Physics in Medicine emphasizes careful individualization of cancer radiotherapy for the pregnant woman .

239
00:29:29,536 --> 00:29:38,086
In some cases, shielding of the fetus and other safeguards can be employed (Fenig, 2001; Nuyttens, 2002).

240
00:29:38,086 --> 00:29:43,111
However, in other instances, the fetus will be exposed to dangerous

241
00:29:43,276 --> 00:29:48,301
radiation doses, and a carefully designed plan must be improvised .

242
00:29:48,251 --> 00:29:56,188
Models that estimate the fetal dose given during maternal brain radiotherapy or tangential breast irradiation have been developed .

243
00:29:56,188 --> 00:30:05,638
The adverse pregnancy outcomes that occur years after abdominopelvic radiotherapy are detailed in Chapter 66 (p. 1164).

244
00:30:05,638 --> 00:30:09,901
Brent, 2015; Wo, 2009).

245
00:30:09,901 --> 00:30:12,826
# Diagnostic Radiation.

246
00:30:12,826 --> 00:30:18,663
Radiographs To estimate fetal risk, approximate x-ray dosimetry must be known.

247
00:30:18,663 --> 00:30:28,826
According to the American College of Radiology, no single diagnostic procedure results in a radiation dose significant enough to threaten embryofetal well-being .

248
00:30:28,826 --> 00:30:34,551
For standard radiographs, dosimetry is presented in Table 49-7.

249
00:30:34,551 --> 00:30:47,313
In pregnancy, the anterior-posterior-view chest radiograph is the most common study, and fetal exposure is exceptionally small—0.0007 Gy or 70 mrad.

250
00:30:47,313 --> 00:30:59,038
The dose with one abdominal radiograph is higher—0.001 Gy or 100 mrad— because the embryo or fetus lies directly in the x-ray beam path.

251
00:30:59,038 --> 00:31:07,776
The standard intravenous pyelogram may exceed 0.005 Gy or 500 mrad because of several exposures.

252
00:31:07,776 --> 00:31:19,751
The one-shot pyelogram described in Chapter 56 (p. 999) is useful when urolithiasis or other causes of urinary tract obstruction are unproven by sonography.

253
00:31:19,751 --> 00:31:32,838
Mammography and most “trauma series” radiographs of an extremity, skull, or rib deliver low doses because of distance from the fetus (Cepeda-Martins, 2021; Shakerian, 2015).

254
00:31:32,838 --> 00:31:48,113
Fluoroscopy and Angiography Dosimetry calculations are much more difficult with these procedures because of variations in the number of radiographs obtained, total fluoroscopy time, and fluoroscopy time during which the fetus lies in the radiation field.

255
00:31:48,113 --> 00:31:52,426
As shown in Table 49-8, the range varies.

256
00:31:52,426 --> 00:32:04,063
Although the FDA limits the exposure rate for conventional fluoroscopy such as barium studies, special-purpose systems such as angiography units have the potential for much higher exposure.

257
00:32:04,063 --> 00:32:06,601
Table 49-8.

258
00:32:06,601 --> 00:32:19,363
Estimated X-Ray Doses to the Uterus/Embryo from Common Fluoroscopic Procedures Angiography and vascular embolization may occasionally be necessary for trauma and for serious maternal disorders.

259
00:32:19,363 --> 00:32:25,313
As before, a greater distance from the embryo or fetus lowers the exposure and risk.

260
00:32:25,313 --> 00:32:36,963
Computed Tomography In a recent report describing nearly 3.5 million pregnancies, the use of CT imaging from 1996 to 2016 increased fivefold .

261
00:32:36,963 --> 00:32:45,388
These studies are usually performed by obtaining a spiral of 360-degree images that are postprocessed in multiple planes.

262
00:32:45,388 --> 00:32:50,076
Of these, the axial image remains the most frequently obtained.

263
00:32:50,076 --> 00:32:56,913
Multidetector CT (MDCT) images are now standard for common clinical indications.

264
00:32:56,913 --> 00:33:06,688
The most recent detectors have more channels, and these multidetector protocols may result in higher dosimetry compared with prior CT imaging techniques.

265
00:33:06,688 --> 00:33:10,676
Several imaging parameters have an effect on exposure .

266
00:33:10,676 --> 00:33:19,176
These include pitch, kilovoltage, tube current, collimation, slice number, tube rotation, and total acquisition time.

267
00:33:19,176 --> 00:33:26,351
If a study is performed with and without contrast, the dose is doubled because twice as many images are obtained.

268
00:33:26,351 --> 00:33:31,901
Fetal exposure also varies with maternal size and fetal size and position.

269
00:33:31,901 --> 00:33:38,713
As with plain radiography, the closer the target area is to the fetus, the greater the delivered dose.

270
00:33:38,713 --> 00:33:48,476
Cranial CT imaging for evaluation of neurological disorders and eclampsia is the most frequently performed CT study in gravidas (Chaps.

271
00:33:48,476 --> 00:33:54,388
41, p. 718 and 63, p. 1126).

272
00:33:54,388 --> 00:34:02,938
Nonenhanced CT scanning is often used to detect acute hemorrhage within the epidural, subdural, or subarachnoid spaces .

273
00:34:02,938 --> 00:34:07,501
Radiation dosage is negligible because of distance from the fetus .

274
00:34:07,501 --> 00:34:10,138
Figure 49-3.

275
00:34:10,138 --> 00:34:19,926
An image from a noncontrast computed tomography head study demonstrates a large right-sided frontoparietal temporal intraparenchymal hematoma (H ).

276
00:34:19,926 --> 00:34:25,351
The midline (arrow) is shifted to the left due to mass effect from the hematoma.

277
00:34:25,351 --> 00:34:29,238
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Amanda Zofkie.

278
00:34:29,238 --> 00:34:32,538
Abdominal procedures are more problematic.

279
00:34:32,538 --> 00:34:43,626
Hurwitz and associates (2006) employed a 16-channel multidetector scanner to calculate fetal exposure at 0 and 3 months’ gestation using a phantom model.

280
00:34:43,626 --> 00:34:46,376
Calculations were made for three

281
00:34:46,540 --> 00:34:50,090
commonly requested procedures in pregnant women .

282
00:34:50,040 --> 00:35:00,440
The study showed their pulmonary embolism protocol has the same dosimetry exposure as the ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) lung scan discussed subsequently.

283
00:35:00,440 --> 00:35:09,002
Although the appendicitis protocol has the highest radiation exposure, it is very useful clinically when MR imaging is not available.

284
00:35:09,002 --> 00:35:20,977
Using a greater pitch markedly decreases the dosimetry and yields a sensitivity of 92 percent, a specificity of 99 percent, and a negativepredictive value of 99 percent .

285
00:35:20,977 --> 00:35:26,152
This is discussed further in Chapter 55 (p. 987).

286
00:35:26,152 --> 00:35:33,552
For suspected urolithiasis, the multidetector-scan protocol is used if sonography is nondiagnostic.

287
00:35:33,552 --> 00:35:43,102
White and coworkers (2007) identified urolithiasis in 13 of 20 women with gestations aged an average of 26 weeks.

288
00:35:43,102 --> 00:35:55,802
Last, as shown in Figure 49-4, abdominal tomography is performed if indicated in the pregnant woman with severe trauma (Herfel, 2018; Shakerian, 2015).

289
00:35:55,802 --> 00:35:58,390
Table 49-9.

290
00:35:58,390 --> 00:36:08,527
Estimated Radiation Dosimetry with 16-Channel Multidetector Computed-Tomographic (MDCT) Imaging Protocols Figure 49-4.

291
00:36:08,527 --> 00:36:14,015
This woman in her third trimester was involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident.

292
00:36:14,015 --> 00:36:21,952
A. Maximum intensity projection acquired for maternal indications readily identifies fetal skull fractures (arrows).

293
00:36:21,952 --> 00:36:31,015
B. 3-D reformatted CT image in a bone algorithm demonstrates the fetal skeleton from data acquired during the maternal examination.

294
00:36:31,015 --> 00:36:34,515
Again, the arrow marks one fracture site.

295
00:36:34,515 --> 00:36:40,590
Reproduced with permission from Bailey AA, Twickler DM: Perioperative imaging.

296
00:36:40,590 --> 00:36:49,902
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

297
00:36:49,902 --> 00:36:54,390
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

298
00:36:54,390 --> 00:36:57,740
Photo contributor: Dr. Travis Browning.

299
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:03,977
Most experience with chest CT imaging is with cases of suspected pulmonary embolism.

300
00:37:03,977 --> 00:37:15,727
Historically, pulmonary scintigraphy—the V/Q scan— was recommended for pregnant women by 70 percent of radiologists and chest CT angiography by 30 percent .

301
00:37:15,727 --> 00:37:26,990
However, most currently agree that multidetector-CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) has improved accuracy because of increasingly faster acquisition times .

302
00:37:26,990 --> 00:37:36,202
Despite advances in technology, scintigraphy is still recommended by the American Thoracic Society for gravidas with a normal chest x-ray .

303
00:37:36,202 --> 00:37:50,515
A higher use rate for CTPA has been reported, and dosimetry similar to that with V/Q scintigraphy has been emphasized (Brenner, 2007; Greer, 2015; Tromeur, 2019).

304
00:37:50,515 --> 00:37:58,690
The rapid turnaround time with current CTPA protocols at most hospitals has advanced its selection as the preferred modality .

305
00:37:58,690 --> 00:38:08,052
The algorithm in the YEARS study describes the use of D-dimer values and clinical criteria to define a subgroup of gravidas warranting further imaging.

306
00:38:08,052 --> 00:38:16,990
Thus, the number of indicated CTPA studies resulting from this algorithm is markedly reduced (van der Pol, 2019).

307
00:38:16,990 --> 00:38:26,990
In pregnancy, although a negative D-dimer test is helpful, these levels may be normally elevated with increasing gestation and certain maternal complications .

308
00:38:26,990 --> 00:38:37,927
Of other aspects in study selection, the fetal radiation doses are lower with CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) compared with the V/Q scan.

309
00:38:37,927 --> 00:38:45,990
However, maternal chest radiation doses are substantially higher with CTPA (van Mens, 2017).

310
00:38:45,990 --> 00:39:01,090
The most recent ongoing Optimised Computed Tomography Pulmonary Angiography in Pregnancy Quality and Safety (OPTICA) study is a prospective trial using a uniform low-dose CTPA protocol to enable definitive recommendations.

311
00:39:01,090 --> 00:39:11,215
Until results are known, our reduced-exposure CTPA protocol is recommended as the initial preferred imaging modality in suspected pulmonary embolism .

312
00:39:11,215 --> 00:39:16,440
CT pelvimetry is used by some before attempting breech vaginal delivery .

313
00:39:16,440 --> 00:39:30,102
The fetal dose approaches 0.015 Gy or 1.5 rad, but use of a low-exposure technique may reduce this to 0.0025 Gy or 0.25 rad.

314
00:39:30,102 --> 00:39:35,715
Radiographic Contrast Agents These can be given intravenously or taken orally.

315
00:39:35,715 --> 00:39:40,940
Intravenous contrast agents are considered category B by the FDA.

316
00:39:40,940 --> 00:39:47,427
These agents are iodinated and of low osmolality, and thus they cross the placenta to the fetus.

317
00:39:47,427 --> 00:39:54,565
With watersoluble iodinated contrast, no cases of neonatal hypothyroidism or other adverse

318
00:39:54,724 --> 00:39:57,211
effects have been documented .

319
00:39:57,161 --> 00:40:06,549
Oral contrast preparations, typically containing iodine or barium, have minimal systemic absorption, and are unlikely to affect the fetus.

320
00:40:06,549 --> 00:40:15,424
Nuclear Medicine Studies These studies are performed by “tagging” a radioactive element to a carrier that can be injected, inhaled, or swallowed.

321
00:40:15,424 --> 00:40:25,761
For example, the radioisotope technetium-99m (Tc-99m) may be tagged to red blood cells, sulfur colloid, or pertechnetate.

322
00:40:25,761 --> 00:40:30,549
The method used to tag the agent determines fetal radiation exposure.

323
00:40:30,549 --> 00:40:39,486
The amount of placental transfer is obviously important, but maternal renal clearance is also factored because of fetal proximity to her bladder.

324
00:40:39,486 --> 00:40:48,224
Measurement of radioactive technetium is based on its decay, and the units used are the curie (Ci) or the Becquerel (Bq).

325
00:40:48,224 --> 00:40:52,736
Dosimetry is usually expressed in millicuries (mCi).

326
00:40:52,736 --> 00:41:02,024
The effective tissue dose is expressed in sievert units (Sv) with conversion of 1 Sv = 100 rem = 100 rad .

327
00:41:02,024 --> 00:41:13,374
Depending on the physical and biochemical properties of a radioisotope, an average fetal exposure can be calculated (Wagner, 1997; Zanzonico, 2000).

328
00:41:13,374 --> 00:41:20,774
Commonly used radiopharmaceuticals and estimated absorbed fetal doses are given in Table 49-10.

329
00:41:20,774 --> 00:41:24,724
The radionuclide dose should be kept as low as possible .

330
00:41:24,724 --> 00:41:31,624
Exposures vary with gestational age and are greatest earlier in pregnancy for most radiopharmaceuticals.

331
00:41:31,624 --> 00:41:37,224
One exception is the later effect of iodine-131 on the fetal thyroid .

332
00:41:37,224 --> 00:41:39,786
Table 49-10.

333
00:41:39,786 --> 00:41:55,674
Radiopharmaceuticals Used in Nuclear Medicine Studies In a V/Q scan, perfusion is measured with injected Tc-99m macroaggregated albumin, and ventilation is measured with inhaled xenon127 or xenon-133.

334
00:41:55,674 --> 00:42:02,661
Fetal exposure with either is negligible (Chan, 2002; Mountford, 1997).

335
00:42:02,661 --> 00:42:09,861
Thyroid scanning with iodine-123 or iodine-131 seldom is indicated in pregnancy.

336
00:42:09,861 --> 00:42:15,036
However, with the trace diagnostic doses used, fetal risk is minimal.

337
00:42:15,036 --> 00:42:25,099
In contrast, therapeutic doses of radioiodine to treat maternal Graves disease or thyroid cancer may cause fetal thyroid ablation and cretinism.

338
00:42:25,099 --> 00:42:35,599
The sentinel lymphoscintigram is a commonly used preoperative study in nonpregnant women to detect the axillary lymph node most likely to have metastases from breast cancer.

339
00:42:35,599 --> 00:42:44,449
Tc-99m–sulfur colloid is used in this diagnostic study (Newman, 2007; Spanheimer, 2009).

340
00:42:44,449 --> 00:42:54,324
The calculated dose approximates 0.014 mSv or 1.4 mrad, which should not preclude its safe use during pregnancy.

341
00:42:54,324 --> 00:42:56,186
Sonography.

342
00:42:56,186 --> 00:43:02,711
The development of sonography for study of the fetus and mother is one of the greater achievements in obstetrics.

343
00:43:02,711 --> 00:43:07,486
The technique has become virtually indispensable in everyday practice.

344
00:43:07,486 --> 00:43:14,811
Its wide-ranging clinical uses are further discussed in Chapters 14 and 15 and in other sections of this book.

345
00:43:14,811 --> 00:43:17,386
Magnetic Resonance Imaging.

346
00:43:17,386 --> 00:43:28,986
Magnetic resonance technology does not use ionizing radiation, and its use in pregnancy is constantly evolving (Gopireddy, 2021; Mervak, 2019).

347
00:43:28,986 --> 00:43:42,186
Advantages include sharp resolution at soft-tissue interfaces, ability to characterize tissue composition, and acquisition of images in any plane— particularly axial, sagittal, and coronal.

348
00:43:42,186 --> 00:43:52,536
A portion of Chapter 14 (p. 263) is devoted to mechanisms that generate MR images, and imaging examples are provided throughout this book.

349
00:43:52,536 --> 00:43:54,636
# Safety.

350
00:43:54,636 --> 00:44:02,311
The update of the expert panel on MR safety of the ACR was summarized by Kanal and colleagues (2013).

351
00:44:02,311 --> 00:44:07,649
The panel concluded that no harmful human effects are reported from MR imaging.

352
00:44:07,649 --> 00:44:16,111
Similar conclusions were reached by the International Society for Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology (2017).

353
00:44:16,111 --> 00:44:26,161
When operated within standardized limits, maternal and fetal imaging can be safely performed at clinical magnet strengths—3 tesla (T) and below.

354
00:44:26,161 --> 00:44:45,661
MR imaging can be used regardless of trimester: (1) if the information cannot be obtained with another nonionizing modality, namely sonography; (2) if the study results will guide maternal or fetal management during pregnancy; and (3) if the imaging cannot be delayed until the woman is no longer pregnant.

355
00:44:45,661 --> 00:44:53,524
The decision to use a magnetic field strength >1.5 T may be made for specific maternal indications.

356
00:44:53,524 --> 00:44:57,186
Early work also suggests imaging at 3 T

357
00:44:57,340 --> 00:45:04,615
is safe and it improves fetal assessment (Chartier, 2019; Victoria, 2016).

358
00:45:04,565 --> 00:45:10,490
No demonstrable fetal heart rate pattern changes occur during MR imaging of gravidas .

359
00:45:10,490 --> 00:45:20,190
Last, studies evaluating children exposed in utero have shown no deleterious effects (Kok, 2004; Reeves, 2010).

360
00:45:20,190 --> 00:45:39,677
Contraindications to MR imaging include internal cardiac pacemakers, neurostimulators, implanted defibrillators and infusion pumps, cochlear implants, shrapnel or other metal in biologically sensitive areas, some intracranial aneurysm clips, and any metallic foreign body in the eye.

361
00:45:39,677 --> 00:45:50,740
Of more than 51,000 nonpregnant patients scheduled for MR imaging, one study found that only 0.4 percent had an absolute contraindication to the procedure .

362
00:45:50,740 --> 00:45:53,452
# Contrast Agents.

363
00:45:53,452 --> 00:45:59,415
Several different elemental gadolinium chelates are used to create paramagnetic contrast.

364
00:45:59,415 --> 00:46:06,015
These cross the placenta and are found in the fetus and placenta and are concentrated in amnionic fluid .

365
00:46:06,015 --> 00:46:15,627
In doses approximately 10 times the normal human dose, a gadolinium-based contrast agent caused slight developmental delay in rabbit fetuses.

366
00:46:15,627 --> 00:46:20,340
Inadvertant fetal exposure usually occurs in early pregnancy .

367
00:46:20,340 --> 00:46:29,702
The American College of Radiology cites a study of 26 women given a gadolinium derivative in the first trimester without adverse fetal effects .

368
00:46:29,702 --> 00:46:46,827
Despite this, routine use of gadolinium is not recommended unless potential benefits outweigh fetal risks (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017; American College of Radiology, 2020; Briggs, 2017).

369
00:46:46,827 --> 00:46:57,490
This recommendation stems from a possible dissociation of the toxic gadolinium ion from its ligand within amnionic fluid and thus potential prolonged exposure of the fetus.

370
00:46:57,490 --> 00:47:00,327
# Maternal Indications.

371
00:47:00,327 --> 00:47:07,240
In some cases, MR imaging may complement CT, and in others, MR imaging is preferable .

372
00:47:07,240 --> 00:47:13,990
MR imaging is also a superb tool to evaluate the maternal abdomen and retroperitoneal space.

373
00:47:13,990 --> 00:47:25,627
One example is evaluation of right lower quadrant pain in pregnancy, specifically with suspected appendicitis (Aguilera, 2018; Tsai, 2017).

374
00:47:25,627 --> 00:47:39,102
It can aid detection and localization of adrenal, renal, and gastrointestinal lesions as well as pelvic masses in pregnancy (Boyd, 2012; Raj, 2020; Tica, 2013).

375
00:47:39,102 --> 00:47:47,452
Maternal central nervous system abnormalities, such as brain tumors or spinal trauma, are more clearly seen with MR imaging.

376
00:47:47,452 --> 00:47:52,327
This makes it invaluable in the diagnosis of neurological emergencies .

377
00:47:52,327 --> 00:48:09,565
Other modalities include MR urography for renal urolithiasis and cardiac MR for investigating normal physiology, complex defects, and cardiomyopathies (Mullins, 2012; Nelson, 2015; Stewart, 2016).

378
00:48:09,565 --> 00:48:15,177
The application of cardiovascular MR imaging in pregnant women is expanding .

379
00:48:15,177 --> 00:48:20,227
MR imaging helps also evaluate many pregnancy-specific disorders.

380
00:48:20,227 --> 00:48:26,477
It is chosen by many to determine the degree and extent of invasion in placenta accreta spectrum .

381
00:48:26,477 --> 00:48:39,990
As discussed in Chapter 40 (p. 701), MR imaging has provided important insights into the pathophysiology of preeclampsia (Nelander, 2018; Zeeman, 2014).

382
00:48:39,990 --> 00:48:58,990
As discussed in Chapter 37 (p. 656), CT and MR imaging are useful for puerperal infection evaluation, but MR imaging provides better visualization of the bladder flap area following cesarean delivery (Brown, 1999; Twickler, 1997).

383
00:48:58,990 --> 00:49:04,790
Last, preliminary studies of placental function with MR imaging are promising .

384
00:49:04,790 --> 00:49:07,502
# Fetal Indications.

385
00:49:07,502 --> 00:49:16,140
Fetal MR imaging provides a complement to sonography (Laifer-Narin, 2007; Sandrasegaran, 2006).

386
00:49:16,140 --> 00:49:26,377
According to Zaretsky and associates (2003a), MR imaging can be used to display almost all elements of the standard fetal anatomical survey.

387
00:49:26,377 --> 00:49:33,352
Moreover, the quality of three-dimensional anatomical reconstruction with MR fetal imaging is superb .

388
00:49:33,352 --> 00:49:42,190
The most frequent fetal indications are evaluation of complex abnormalities of the brain, chest, and genitourinary systems .

389
00:49:42,190 --> 00:49:52,277
Reichel (2003) and Twickler (2002) and their colleagues have validated its use for fetal central nervous system anomalies and biometry .

390
00:49:52,277 --> 00:50:04,027
Others have described MR imaging of fetuses with suprarenal masses or with renal anomalies and oligohydramnios (Castro, 2019; Hawkins, 2008).

391
00:50:04,027 --> 00:50:07,852
Fetal weight estimation may be more accurate with MR

392
00:50:08,020 --> 00:50:14,795
imaging than with sonography (Kadji, 2019; Zaretsky, 2003b).

393
00:50:14,745 --> 00:50:23,357
And MR imaging has been shown to accurately identify fetal anemia requiring transfusions (Jørgensen, 2019).

394
00:50:23,357 --> 00:50:26,107
Figure 49-5.

395
00:50:26,107 --> 00:50:29,570
Nullipara with a 27 weeks’ gestation.

396
00:50:29,570 --> 00:50:38,332
A. Axial T2-weighted MR image shows mild fetal unilateral ventriculomegaly involving the left lateral ventricle (arrow).

397
00:50:38,332 --> 00:50:46,657
B. Sagittal T2-weighted MR image demonstrates normal development of the corpus callosum (arrowheads) and vermis (arrow).

398
00:50:46,657 --> 00:50:53,120
Fetal movement is problematic for MR imaging, but faster acquisitions eliminate the problem.

399
00:50:53,120 --> 00:51:05,857
Morphology is primarily assessed with fast T2weighted sequences such as half-Fourier acquisition single shot turbo spin echo (HASTE) or single shot fast spin echo (SSFSE).

400
00:51:05,857 --> 00:51:15,232
Fetal indications and findings of MR imaging are discussed more extensively in Chapter 14 (p. 266) and throughout this book.

401
00:51:15,232 --> 00:51:17,632
Imaging During Pregnancy.

402
00:51:17,632 --> 00:51:29,632
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) has reviewed the effects of radiographic, sonographic, and magnetic-resonance exposure during pregnancy.

403
00:51:29,632 --> 00:51:34,020
Its suggested guidelines are shown in Table 4911.

404
00:51:34,020 --> 00:51:36,670
